The laws of thermodynamics are fundamental principles that govern energy, heat, and work in physical systems.

## Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics

If two systems are each in thermal equilibrium with a third system, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. 

This law establishes the concept of temperature and makes thermometers possible—it tells us that temperature is a meaningful, measurable property.

## First Law of Thermodynamics

**Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another.** 

The total energy of an isolated system remains constant. This is essentially the law of conservation of energy. In practical terms: the change in internal energy of a system equals the heat added to the system minus the work done by the system.

## Second Law of Thermodynamics

**In any energy transfer or transformation, the total entropy (disorder) of an isolated system always increases over time.**

Heat naturally flows from hotter to cooler objects, never the reverse without external work. This law explains why perpetual motion machines are impossible and why processes have a natural direction—a broken egg won't spontaneously reassemble itself.

## Third Law of Thermodynamics

**As temperature approaches absolute zero (0 Kelvin or -273.15°C), the entropy of a perfect crystal approaches zero.**

This means absolute zero is unreachable in practice, though we can get extremely close. It provides an absolute reference point for measuring entropy.

---

## Implications

These laws have profound implications—they govern everything from engines and refrigerators to the evolution of the universe itself, explaining why stars burn out and why biological systems require constant energy input to maintain their organization.
